  "cli_result_text": "Submitted. Summary of the dependency reality established from manifests:\n\n**Babel \u2192 Next.js edge exists** (`packages/next/package.json` `devDependencies`: `@babel/core`, `@babel/traverse`, `@babel/types`, `@babel/generator`, `@babel/preset-env/react/typescript`, `@babel/eslint-parser`, etc.). Next.js ncc-bundles these into `next/dist/compiled/babel/*` at its own build time (`packages/next/taskfile-ncc.js`) and drives real AST traversal against them in `packages/next/src/build/babel/plugins/*` (e.g. `next-ssg-transform.ts` switches on `decl.node.type`, `commonjs.ts`, `next-page-config.ts`) \u2014 genuinely exposed to a new `DecoratorMetadata` node.\n\n**Babel \u2192 webpack edge does NOT exist at runtime**, contrary to what the naive \"webpack uses babel-loader\" assumption would suggest: `package.json`'s `@babel/core`/`@babel/preset-react`/`babel-loader` are all `devDependencies` (used for webpack's own lint/test fixtures), and `lib/javascript/JavascriptParser.js` parses source with `acorn`, not Babel. webpack never touches a Babel AST object directly \u2014 babel-loader (not in this workspace) hands webpack transformed source text, not AST nodes.\n\n**Next.js \u2192 webpack edge exists** (`packages/next/package.json`: `webpack: \"5.86.0\"`) but is irrelevant to this change, since webpack's own parsing path is acorn-based.\n\n**Bonus nuance**: Next.js's *default* compiler is SWC (Rust, `packages/next/src/build/swc/options.ts`, separate `decoratorMetadata` support), not Babel \u2014 the Babel loader path is only exercised when a project supplies a custom `.babelrc`. And inside Babel itself, `@babel/parser`'s `package.json` has no dependency on `@babel/types`, so the parser and types packages must be updated in lockstep by convention, not by any manifest edge.",
